## Overview
This question evaluates understanding of inter-thread synchronization, producer-consumer patterns, and low-level data structure design in operating system environments.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffer with head/tail indices and wraparound handling
- Mutexes and condition variables for blocking operations and wakeups
- Memory visibility guarantees via compiler and hardware memory barriers
- Correctness and performance characteristics with multiple concurrent producers

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
    char data[LOG_SIZE];
    int valid;
} slot_t;

slot_t buffer[N];
int head = 0;
int tail = 0;
int count = 0;

mutex_t mtx;
cond_t not_full;
cond_t not_empty;

void enqueue(const char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count >= N)
        cond_wait(&not_full, &mtx);
    buffer[tail % N] = *(slot_t *)record;
    tail++;
    count++;
    cond_signal(&not_empty);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}

void dequeue(char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count == 0)
        cond_wait(&not_empty, &mtx);
    *(slot_t *)record = buffer[head % N];
    head++;
    count--;
    cond_signal(&not_full);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Correctness**: Verify no data loss or corruption with single producer/consumer
2. **Blocking**: Confirm enqueue blocks when buffer is full, dequeue blocks when empty
3. **Throughput**: Measure records/second with 1, 4, 8, and 16 concurrent producers
4. **Stress**: Run sustained test to detect race conditions and memory leaks

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about scalable scheduler design under high concurrency and NUMA-aware memory behavior, reflecting real-world cloud and fintech workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-CPU run queues enabling lock-free enqueue/dequeue operations
- Dynamic priority boosting based on wait-time and latency telemetry
- NUMA-aware task placement to minimize memory access latency
- Global I/O-priority bitmap for efficient priority scanning
- Lock-free enqueue using compare-and-swap (CAS) primitives

## Validation Approach
Fairness and throughput under contention can be validated through:
1. **Fairness testing**: Measure wait time distribution across task types using synthetic workloads with controlled I/O and CPU-bound ratios
2. **Throughput benchmarking**: Run high-concurrency workloads and measure tasks completed per second
3. **NUMA locality analysis**: Track remote vs. local memory accesses using hardware counters
4. **Starvation detection**: Verify I/O-bound tasks receive CPU time within bounded latency under sustained CPU contention
5. **Load balance profiling**: Monitor task migration frequency and queue depth across CPUs

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Task {
    atomic_int priority;
    u64 wait_time;
    u64 last_io_latency;
    struct Task *next;
} Task;

typedef struct PerCpuQueue {
    Task *head;
    atomic_T tail;
} PerCpuQueue;

void enqueue(Task *t) {
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[smp_processor_id()];
    Task *old_tail = atomic_load(&q->tail);
    do {
        t->next = old_tail;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange(&q->tail, &old_tail, t));
}

Task *dequeue(void) {
    int cpu = find_highest_priority_cpu();
    PerCpuQueue *q = &per_cpu_queues[cpu];
    return atomic_fetch_head(&q->head);
}

void rebalance(void) {
    for_each_cpu(cpu) {
        if (queue_depth(cpu) > threshold)
            migrate_to_neighbor(cpu);
    }
}
```

